          {
            title: 'Seotda',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Seotda is a traditional Korean two-card game played with 20 Hwatu cards; players form ranked combinations (GwangTtaeng, Ttaeng, Geut) and bet. LA Seotda is a modern variant with a 52-card deck and poker-style elements.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Country/Region of Origin: South Korea\nTime Period: Traditional; Hwatu/Go-Stop culture\nHistorical Development: Traditional Korean two-card game played with 20 cards from a Hwatu deck (flower cards); combines luck and strategy with GwangTtaeng and Ttaeng hand rankings.\nCultural Significance: Popular social and gambling game in Korea; LA Seotda is a modern poker-influenced variant.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Minimum Players: 2\nMaximum Players: 6\nOptimal Players: 4\nPartnerships: Optional',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Type: Standard 52-card deck\nNumber of Decks: 1\nJokers: Not included',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard 52-card deck\nScore sheet (optional)\nTable space',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Cards per Player: 2\nDealing Pattern: Clockwise, one card at a time or two to each player.\nDealer rotates; cards dealt face down.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
